    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Ripe papaya and pineapple are both safe to enjoy in moderation during pregnancy. The one to avoid is raw or unripe (green) papaya. The wider belief that these are dangerous "heat" foods is largely a myth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The real caution is only about unripe papaya, which contains a milky latex and an enzyme called papain that can act as a mild uterine stimulant. As papaya ripens this latex all but disappears, so fully ripe papaya is safe and even nourishing, rich in folate, vitamin C and fibre. Pineapple is blamed for its enzyme bromelain, but the amount in the flesh you eat is tiny, mostly sitting in the core, and is broken down by digestion; you would need to eat many whole pineapples at once to have any effect. The idea that "garam" or heaty foods cause harm has no strong scientific basis, and leading bodies like FOGSI (the Federation of Obstetric and Gynaecological Societies of India) and ACOG (the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists) do not single these fruits out. Most miscarriages are due to chromosomal chance, not food.',
