  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-022',
    number: 72,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Why do I have so much saliva in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Producing more saliva than usual, sometimes enough that you want to spit it out, is a harmless and fairly common early pregnancy symptom. It is called ptyalism, and it usually settles as the first trimester ends.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'It is linked to pregnancy hormones and to nausea, since feeling queasy can make you swallow less, so saliva pools. It often goes hand in hand with morning sickness and tends to ease by the second trimester.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is no need to treat it. Sipping water through the day, sucking on something minty or citrusy, chewing sugar-free gum, and keeping up your usual brushing can all make it more manageable. Staying hydrated matters, especially if you are also being sick.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention it if it comes with severe vomiting or signs of dehydration like very dark urine.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'It is an odd and little-talked-about symptom, but it is harmless and, for most women, short-lived.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Extra saliva (ptyalism) is harmless and common early on.',
        'It is linked to hormones and nausea.',
        'It usually eases by the second trimester.',
        'Sip water, chew gum, and keep up oral care.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-019',
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-003',
      'PREG-BODY-017',
