  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-023',
    number: 73,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Why is my skin getting darker in patches during pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'All trimesters (often T2 onward)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Darker patches on the face and a dark line down the middle of the belly are common, normal skin changes in pregnancy. They are caused by hormones increasing the pigment in your skin, and they usually fade after birth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Rising pregnancy hormones prompt your skin to make more melanin, the pigment that gives skin its colour. This can show as brownish patches on the cheeks, forehead or upper lip, often called the "mask of pregnancy" or melasma, a dark vertical line on the lower belly called the linea nigra, and darkening of the nipples. It is more noticeable on Indian and deeper skin tones, and sunlight makes facial patches darker.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'These changes are harmless and usually lighten on their own within months of giving birth. Daily sun protection, a broad-spectrum sunscreen, a hat and shade, helps keep facial patches from darkening. It is best not to use strong skin-lightening creams in pregnancy; if the pigmentation bothers you, your doctor or a dermatologist can advise on safe options.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Check with your doctor before using any skin-lightening treatment, and mention any patch that is itchy, painful, raised or changing, which is different from ordinary pigmentation.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'These marks are a temporary signature of pregnancy, not something to fix, and for most women they quietly fade with time.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Facial patches (melasma) and a belly line (linea nigra) are normal.',
        'Caused by hormones raising skin pigment.',
        'More noticeable on deeper skin tones; sun darkens facial patches.',
        'Usually fade within months after birth.',
        'Use sun protection; avoid strong lightening creams.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-010',
      'PREG-CANI-DO-004',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-024',
    number: 74,
    month: 2,
    question: 'My pregnancy symptoms come and go, or have faded. Is that normal?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'It is completely normal for early pregnancy symptoms to come and go, feel stronger some days and barely there on others, or ease off as you near the end of the first trimester. Symptoms fluctuating does not mean anything is wrong.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Symptoms like nausea and breast tenderness are driven by hormones that naturally rise and dip day to day, so they wax and wane. Around weeks 11 to 14, many symptoms fade as the placenta takes over hormone production, which is a normal and welcome shift rather than a worry. Some women have few symptoms throughout, and that too is normal.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Try not to read too much into a quieter day; it is not a reliable sign of how the pregnancy is going. The one thing worth a call is a sudden, complete loss of all symptoms very early on, together with bleeding or strong cramping, which is worth checking simply for peace of mind.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Contact your doctor if symptoms vanish suddenly and completely in early pregnancy alongside bleeding or strong cramping, so it can be checked calmly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Symptom-watching can become its own source of worry; gentler days are usually just your hormones settling, not a warning.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Early symptoms naturally come and go.',
        'Hormone levels rise and dip day to day.',
        'Many symptoms ease around weeks 11 to 14 as the placenta takes over.',
        'Few symptoms can be perfectly normal too.',
        'Check only if all symptoms vanish suddenly with bleeding or cramping.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-019',
      'PREG-EMOTION-001',
      'PREG-HEALTH-012',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-EAT-004',
    number: 75,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Can I eat fish and seafood during pregnancy?',
    category: 'Food (Can I > Eat)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'In most pregnancies you can eat fish, and it is good for you and your baby, as long as you choose low-mercury fish, cook it well, and keep to about two servings a week. Only a few large fish, and all raw or undercooked seafood, are best avoided.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Fish is an excellent source of protein and omega-3 fats, including DHA (docosahexaenoic acid), which supports your baby\'s brain and eye development. The caution is about mercury, which builds up in large, long-lived fish and can affect a baby\'s developing nervous system, and about raw seafood, which carries an infection risk. So the guidance is to enjoy smaller, low-mercury fish and avoid the high-mercury ones.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Good low-mercury choices widely available in India include pomfret, rawas (Indian salmon), Indian mackerel (bangda), sardines, and freshwater fish like rohu and catla, plus well-cooked prawns. Avoid high-mercury fish like shark, swordfish, king mackerel and tilefish, and avoid raw fish such as sushi and any undercooked shellfish. Cook fish thoroughly until it flakes.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor or a dietitian for advice if you are unsure which local fish are safe, or if you have specific dietary concerns.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Fish is a genuinely good food in pregnancy, so the aim is choosing wisely, not giving it up.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Fish is nourishing; aim for about two servings a week.',
        'Choose low-mercury fish and cook them well.',
        'Good Indian options: pomfret, rawas, bangda, sardines, rohu, catla, cooked prawns.',
        'Avoid shark, swordfish, king mackerel and tilefish.',
        'Avoid all raw or undercooked seafood.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-002',
      'PREG-BABY-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-EAT-005',
    number: 76,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Does saffron (kesar) milk make the baby fairer?',
    category: 'Food (Can I > Eat)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters (extra caution T1)',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'A warm cup of kesar (saffron) milk is a cherished part of many Indian pregnancies, and small culinary amounts are generally considered safe. The popular belief that it makes the baby fairer, though, is a myth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'A baby\'s complexion is set entirely by the genes inherited from both parents, and no food, spice or drink can change it. Saffron does not affect it in any way; every baby\'s skin tone is already decided and perfectly healthy. Saffron itself, in the few strands used in milk or cooking, is fine for most women, though large or medicinal amounts are not advised, as concentrated saffron can overstimulate the womb.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Enjoy kesar milk for its warmth, taste and the calcium and protein the milk provides, if you like it, not to change anything about your baby. Use just a few strands, buy pure saffron rather than cheap dyed versions, and if you are in the first trimester or have a high-risk pregnancy, check with your doctor before taking it regularly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Check with your doctor before regular saffron in the first trimester or a high-risk pregnancy, and avoid large or supplement doses.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Keep the lovely ritual and let go of the myth; your baby\'s complexion is already perfect exactly as it will be.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Small culinary amounts of saffron are generally safe.',
        'It does not make the baby fairer; complexion is genetic.',
        'Avoid large or medicinal doses.',
        'Use a few strands of pure saffron.',
        'Check with your doctor in the first trimester or if high-risk.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-002',
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-003',
      'PREG-CANI-DRINK-004',
    ],
