    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Curd (dahi) is safe and good for you in pregnancy, as long as it is made from pasteurised or properly boiled milk. The common belief that curd or cold foods give the baby a cold is a myth.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Colds are caused by viruses, not by curd or cold food, so eating dahi cannot give you or your baby one. Curd is actually a nourishing choice, providing calcium, protein and gut-friendly probiotics that can also ease the acidity and digestion troubles common in pregnancy. The only real caution is the milk it is made from: avoid loose, raw-milk or street-set curd, which can carry infection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Enjoy a bowl of plain curd, chaas or a lassi daily, choosing home-set curd from boiled milk or sealed branded curd, and going easy on sugary flavoured yogurts. If very cold curd feels uncomfortable, simply let it come to room temperature; that is a comfort choice, not a medical rule.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Mention it only if dairy consistently upsets your stomach, or you have gestational diabetes and want to confirm portions.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'You can honour the affection behind "don\'t eat curd" while trusting the science: dahi is nourishing, not harmful.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Curd is safe and nourishing in pregnancy.',
        'Use pasteurised or boiled-milk curd; avoid raw or street-set.',
        '"Curd causes a cold" is a myth; colds are viral.',
        'It gives calcium, protein and probiotics.',
        'Room-temperature curd is fine if cold feels uncomfortable.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
