  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-TAKE-003',
    number: 84,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Which supplements do I need in pregnancy besides folic acid?',
    category: 'Medicines (Can I > Take)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Take',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Besides folic acid, most pregnancies need iron and calcium, and often vitamin D, with others like vitamin B12, iodine and omega-3 depending on your diet and health. Your doctor decides exactly what you need, so take supplements on their advice rather than choosing your own.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy raises your needs for several nutrients beyond what food alone often provides, especially in India where iron-deficiency anaemia and vitamin D shortfall are common. Iron supports the extra blood you are making, calcium and vitamin D build your baby\'s bones while protecting yours, and omega-3 fats such as DHA (docosahexaenoic acid), along with vitamin B12 and iodine, support your baby\'s brain and development, which matters most if you eat little or no dairy, fish or animal foods. In India, government health centres provide iron, folic acid and calcium free.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Take what your doctor prescribes, at the timing they suggest, since iron and calcium are best not taken at the exact same time, and iron absorbs better with a little vitamin C, like nimbu or amla, and less well with tea or coffee. Do not add extra high-dose supplements on your own, as some, like too much vitamin A, can be harmful. A balanced diet still does much of the work.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Ask your doctor which supplements and doses are right for you, especially if you are vegetarian or vegan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Supplements fill the gaps a good diet cannot always cover; the aim is the right few, not the most.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Beyond folic acid, iron and calcium are usually needed, often vitamin D.',
        'B12, iodine and omega-3 matter more on plant-based diets.',
        'Your doctor decides what and how much.',
        'Space iron and calcium apart; iron likes vitamin C, not tea.',
        'Don\'t self-add high-dose supplements.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-001',
      'PREG-HEALTH-006',
      'PREG-BABY-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-025',
    number: 85,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Why can\'t I sleep well in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Sleep',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Trouble sleeping is common in early pregnancy and, while tiring, is not harmful to your baby. It is usually down to hormones, nausea and extra trips to the bathroom, and it often eases in the second trimester.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Rising progesterone changes your sleep patterns, while nausea, tender breasts, a racing mind and frequent urination all interrupt the night. Anxiety about the pregnancy can add to it. None of this harms your baby, though the tiredness is real and worth taking seriously.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A steady wind-down helps: a regular bedtime, dim lights and less screen time in the hour before bed, cutting caffeine earlier in the day, and something calming like reading, prayer or gentle breathing. Get comfortable on your side with a pillow between your knees, keep water by the bed but ease off fluids just before sleep, and nap in the day if you can. If you lie awake, get up and do something quiet rather than watch the clock. Importantly, do not take any sleep medicine, including over-the-counter or herbal ones, without your doctor.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Speak to your doctor if you sleep very little for several nights, cannot function by day, or feel persistently low or anxious.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Broken sleep is one of early pregnancy\'s quiet challenges; small routines help more than trying to force sleep.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Sleep trouble is common early on and not harmful to the baby.',
        'Caused by hormones, nausea, urination and a busy mind.',
        'A calming routine and less evening screen time help.',
        'Side-sleep with a pillow; nap when you can.',
        'Never take a sleep aid without your doctor.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-005',
      'PREG-CANI-DO-005',
      'PREG-EMOTION-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-026',
    number: 86,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Is it normal to feel breathless or have a racing heart in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Feeling a little breathless or noticing your heart beating faster or harder is common in pregnancy and usually normal, even early on. It comes from the natural changes in your breathing and circulation, though a few signs, noted below, are worth checking.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy hormones make you breathe a bit more deeply, which can feel like you are working harder for air, and your heart beats faster and pumps far more blood than before, which you may notice as a fluttering or pounding. This is your body doing its job of supplying you and your baby. Anaemia, which is common in India, can add to breathlessness, which is one reason it is checked early.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Slow down and rest when it happens, avoid overexerting, and prop yourself up with a pillow when lying down. Staying well hydrated and keeping your iron levels up help too. What is worth a prompt check is breathlessness that comes on suddenly or at rest, chest pain, a very fast or irregular heartbeat, fainting, or blue lips, which point to something other than ordinary pregnancy changes.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor promptly for sudden or severe breathlessness, chest pain, an irregular or racing heartbeat, or fainting.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Mild breathlessness is usually just your body adapting; knowing the few warning signs lets you relax about the rest.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Mild breathlessness and palpitations are common and usually normal.',
        'Caused by deeper breathing and more blood being pumped.',
        'Anaemia can worsen breathlessness.',
        'Rest, hydrate and keep iron levels up.',
        'Get sudden breathlessness, chest pain or fainting checked promptly.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-006',
      'PREG-BODY-012',
      'PREG-HEALTH-013',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-SCAN-022',
    number: 87,
    month: 2,
    question: 'What scans and tests will I have through pregnancy?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Across pregnancy you will have a handful of routine scans and blood tests, spaced at set weeks. In the second month, that means your first scan and booking blood tests; the bigger screening scans come later.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy is monitored on a timeline because different checks are useful at different stages. The usual Indian schedule is: an early dating or viability scan around 6 to 8 weeks; booking blood tests at your first visit (blood count, blood group, thyroid, blood sugar and infection screening); the NT (nuchal translucency) scan with the double marker blood test at 11 to 13 weeks; the detailed anomaly scan, often called the TIFFA (Targeted Imaging for Fetal Anomalies) scan, at 18 to 22 weeks; a glucose test for gestational diabetes at 24 to 28 weeks; and growth scans in the last months if needed. Bodies like FOGSI (the Federation of Obstetric and Gynaecological Societies of India) recommend a series of regular antenatal visits alongside these.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'You do not need to memorise this; your doctor will tell you when each is due, and keeping your reports together in one file helps. The main thing is not to miss the timed windows, since some tests are most accurate only within certain weeks. Most results come back reassuring.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your doctor schedules each scan and test; ask them to explain anything you are unsure about.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'The schedule can look busy, but it is simply a calendar designed to catch quiet issues early, and most of the time it brings reassurance.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Scans and tests follow a timeline across pregnancy.',
        'Month 2: early dating scan and booking blood tests.',
        'NT scan and double marker at 11 to 13 weeks.',
        'Anomaly (TIFFA) scan at 18 to 22 weeks.',
        'Glucose test at 24 to 28 weeks; growth scans later if needed.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-SCAN-020',
      'PREG-TEST-002',
      'PREG-SCAN-021',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-027',
    number: 88,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Is back pain normal in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Lower back ache is common in early pregnancy and usually harmless, even before there is any bump. It is mainly caused by pregnancy hormones softening the ligaments around your pelvis.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The hormone relaxin loosens the joints and ligaments of your pelvis to prepare for later, which can leave your lower back feeling less supported and achy. Early posture changes, tiredness and stress add to it. It often feels like a dull, period-type ache and is rarely a sign of any problem on its own.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Gentle movement usually helps more than bed rest: short walks, prenatal yoga, and simple stretches, along with good posture, supportive low-heeled shoes, and side-sleeping with a pillow between your knees. A warm (not hot) compress can soothe tight muscles, and lifting with your knees rather than your back protects it. For any pain relief, check with your doctor first rather than taking anything yourself.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor if back pain is severe, or comes with fever, painful urination or vaginal bleeding, which need checking.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'An achy back this early can be surprising, but it is usually just your body starting its long, clever preparation.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Early back ache is common and usually harmless.',
        'Caused mainly by relaxin loosening pelvic ligaments.',
        'Gentle movement and stretches help more than rest.',
        'Good posture, supportive shoes and side-sleeping ease it.',
        'Check with your doctor for pain relief, or if there\'s fever or bleeding.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-DO-009',
      'PREG-CANI-DO-005',
      'PREG-BODY-007',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-005',
    number: 89,
    month: 2,
    question: 'I don\'t feel connected to my pregnancy yet. Is that normal?',
    category: 'Emotional Wellbeing',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (mental health, gentle register)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Not feeling an instant bond with your pregnancy is completely normal, and it does not mean anything is wrong or that you will not love your baby. For many women, connection grows gradually.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'In early pregnancy there is little to see or feel, no bump, no movement, and often nausea and tiredness instead, so the baby can feel abstract. Bonding frequently deepens as pregnancy becomes more real, especially once you feel movement later on, and continues to grow after birth. How quickly you feel connected says nothing about how good a parent you will be.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is no need to force a feeling. Small things can help it grow when you are ready, talking or singing to your bump, looking at scan pictures, or simply resting and letting yourself adjust. If instead you feel persistently low, numb or unable to enjoy anything, that is worth sharing with your doctor, as support helps.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Speak to your doctor if you feel persistently low, numb or disconnected in a way that worries you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Love does not always arrive as a lightning bolt; for many parents it grows quietly, and that is just as real.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Not feeling bonded early is normal and common.',
        'Early pregnancy can feel abstract with few signs.',
        'Connection often grows, especially after feeling movement.',
        'It does not predict how good a parent you\'ll be.',
        'Tell your doctor if you feel persistently low or numb.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-EMOTION-001',
      'PREG-EMOTION-003',
      'PREG-GS-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-006',
    number: 90,
    month: 2,
    question: 'How can my partner support me in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Partner & Relationships',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Early pregnancy is a big adjustment, and a partner\'s support, practical and emotional, makes a real difference. The most helpful things are often simple: sharing the load, listening, and showing up.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The first months can bring nausea, exhaustion and mixed emotions, often before there is any visible bump, so support can feel invisible but matters a lot. Feeling understood and helped eases stress, which is good for the mother\'s wellbeing and, in turn, the pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Practical help counts: taking on more chores and cooking (especially when smells trigger nausea), coming along to scans and appointments, and handling errands. So does emotional support: listening without rushing to fix, being patient with mood changes, and learning about what is happening week to week so it feels shared. Deciding together on things like when to tell family keeps you a team. Partners have feelings too, and talking openly helps both of you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'There is nothing medical to check here; if either of you feels persistently low or overwhelmed, your doctor can point you to support.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Pregnancy is a shared journey; small, consistent acts of support usually matter more than grand gestures.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'A partner\'s practical and emotional support matters a lot early on.',
        'Share chores and cooking, especially around nausea.',
        'Come to scans and appointments together.',
        'Listen patiently through mood changes.',
        'Make big decisions as a team.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-EMOTION-001',
      'PREG-EMOTION-008',
      'PREG-BABY-003',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-EAT-008',
    number: 91,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Is it safe to eat paneer and cheese in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Food (Can I > Eat)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Paneer and most cheeses are safe in pregnancy, as long as they are made from pasteurised (or well-boiled) milk. The ones to avoid are unpasteurised, raw-milk versions and mould-ripened soft cheeses, unless you cook them until piping hot.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The concern with dairy is listeria, a bacterium that raw-milk soft cheeses can carry and that is risky in pregnancy. Branded Indian paneer (like Amul or Mother Dairy) and home-set paneer from boiled milk are pasteurised and safe, as are hard cheeses and pasteurised soft cheeses like mozzarella. Loose paneer from an unknown source, and soft mould-ripened cheeses such as brie and blue cheese, are the ones to be careful with. Cooking any of these until steaming hot destroys listeria.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Enjoy paneer freely, it is a great vegetarian source of protein and calcium, choosing branded or home-made from boiled milk. If you are unsure how loose paneer was made, simply cook it well, as in paneer bhurji or a hot gravy. Around 100 grams a day fits nicely into a balanced diet.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'No need to check unless you are lactose intolerant or have specific dietary concerns.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Paneer is one of the easiest, most nourishing foods to keep on your plate; only the milk source needs a second thought.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Paneer and most cheeses are safe if from pasteurised or boiled milk.',
        'Branded and home-set (boiled-milk) paneer are safe.',
        'Avoid raw-milk paneer and soft mould-ripened cheeses like brie and blue.',
        'Cooking until piping hot makes any of them safe.',
        'Great vegetarian protein and calcium; about 100 g a day.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
