    question: 'Does spicy food harm the baby?',
    category: 'Food (Can I > Eat)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Spicy food does not harm your baby or cause miscarriage or early labour. It is safe in pregnancy, though it may worsen heartburn or nausea for some women.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'There is no scientific basis for the belief that chillies or masala harm the pregnancy; in many cultures women eat spicy food throughout with no problem. What spice can do is aggravate the acidity, heartburn and queasiness that are already common in pregnancy, simply because it can irritate the stomach.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you enjoy spicy food and it agrees with you, carry on. If it triggers heartburn or nausea, go a little milder, eat smaller portions, and avoid lying down straight after eating. Gentle spices like ginger and turmeric are fine and can even help digestion.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'No need to check about spice unless it consistently triggers severe heartburn.',
