  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-HEALTH-016',
    number: 95,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Burning when I pee: could it be a UTI, and is it serious?',
    category: 'Health & Medical',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Burning or pain when you pee can be a urinary tract infection (UTI), which is common in pregnancy and easily treated, but it does need seeing your doctor promptly rather than waiting it out. Left untreated, a UTI can occasionally travel to the kidneys or affect the pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Pregnancy makes UTIs more likely, because hormones and the growing womb slow the flow of urine, letting bacteria multiply more easily. Typical signs are a burning feeling when you pee, needing to go more often or urgently, cloudy or strong-smelling urine, and lower tummy discomfort. Sometimes there are no symptoms at all, which is why your urine is checked at antenatal visits.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'If you have these symptoms, see your doctor, who will test your urine and, if needed, treat it with a pregnancy-safe antibiotic; do not take any medicine on your own. To lower the risk, drink plenty of water, do not hold urine, wipe front to back, and pass urine after sex.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor promptly for burning, urgency or cloudy urine, and sooner still if you have fever, back pain or blood in your urine.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A UTI is common and very treatable; the one thing not to do is ignore it, since early treatment keeps it simple.',
