        'You can keep your pets during pregnancy, including cats. You do not need to give them away. With cats, the one precaution is to avoid handling cat litter yourself, because cat faeces can carry a parasite called toxoplasma.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Toxoplasmosis is an infection that is usually harmless normally, but caught for the first time in pregnancy it can occasionally affect the baby. The parasite is found in cat faeces, as well as in raw or undercooked meat and soil, not in a cat\'s fur or company. So the risk comes from cleaning the litter tray, gardening, or undercooked meat, rather than from having a pet.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Ask someone else to change the cat litter daily, or if you must do it, wear gloves and wash your hands well afterwards. Keep your cat indoors and feed it cooked or packaged food, avoid stray or new cats, wear gloves when gardening, wash fruits and vegetables, and cook meat thoroughly. Dogs and most other pets pose no special toxoplasmosis risk.',
