    number: 105,
    month: 2,
    question: 'What happens at the early dating (viability) scan?',
    category: 'Scans & Appointments',
    applicability: 'Month 2 (weeks 5 to 8)',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'The early dating or viability scan, usually done around 6 to 8 weeks, confirms three key things: that the pregnancy is in the right place, how many babies there are, and how far along you are. In India it may be done over your tummy (transabdominal) or, especially in these very early weeks, through a slim internal probe (transvaginal) for a clearer picture; your doctor chooses whichever suits your stage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Because the pregnancy is so small this early, an internal (transvaginal) scan, where a slim, covered probe is gently placed in the vagina, often gives the clearest view before about 8 weeks; a little later, a scan over the abdomen usually works well. Either way it checks that the pregnancy is growing inside the womb (not an ectopic pregnancy elsewhere), looks for a heartbeat to confirm viability, counts whether there is one baby or more, and measures the length from head to bottom (the crown-rump length, or CRL) to date the pregnancy. This early measurement is the most reliable way to work out your due date.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'It helps to check when booking whether to come with a full bladder (usual for a tummy scan) or an empty one (for an internal scan). The scan itself is quick. If it is very early, the team may suggest a repeat in a week or so to confirm growth, which is routine rather than a worry. Bring any earlier reports, and ask the sonographer to talk you through what they see.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your doctor arranges this scan and explains the findings; ask if anything is unclear.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'This first glimpse is often an emotional moment; beyond the reassurance, it quietly sets the accurate timeline the rest of your pregnancy will follow.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The dating/viability scan is usually at 6 to 8 weeks.',
        'It confirms location (rules out ectopic), number of babies and dates.',
        'Done over the tummy, or internally (transvaginal) when very early, for a clearer view.',
        'CRL measurement gives the most accurate due date.',
        'A repeat scan if very early is routine, not a red flag.',
      ]),
    ],
