  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-GEN-006',
    number: 106,
    month: 2,
    question: 'How is my due date worked out, and can it change?',
    category: 'Getting Started',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Your due date (estimated delivery date, or EDD) is first worked out from the first day of your last menstrual period (LMP), roughly 40 weeks from then. An early dating scan can adjust it, and yes, it can change, so treat it as an estimate, not a fixed appointment.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The last-period method assumes a regular cycle, so if your cycle is longer, shorter or irregular, or you are unsure of dates, it can be off. That is why the early scan measurement (the crown-rump length) is used to confirm or correct the date; it is the most accurate in the first trimester. Only about one in twenty babies actually arrives on the due date itself; most come in the couple of weeks around it.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Think of the EDD as the centre of a window rather than a deadline. If your scan shifts it by a few days, that is normal and worth noting so your later scans and tests are timed correctly. Counting in weeks, which is how care is scheduled, is more precise than counting in months.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Your doctor sets and, if needed, adjusts your due date from your dates and early scan.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A due date is really a best estimate, a helpful anchor for planning, not a promise your baby has agreed to keep.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'The EDD is about 40 weeks from your last period (LMP).',
        'An early scan (CRL) can confirm or adjust it.',
        'It can change; it is an estimate, not fixed.',
        'Most babies arrive in the weeks around it, not exactly on it.',
        'Care is scheduled in weeks, which is more precise than months.',
