  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-007',
    number: 108,
    month: 2,
    question: 'I can\'t stop worrying about miscarriage. Is that normal?',
    category: 'Emotional Wellbeing',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'Yes (mental health, gentle register)',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Worrying about miscarriage in the first trimester is extremely common and completely understandable, especially before you can feel the baby. It does not mean anything is wrong, and there are gentle ways to carry the worry more lightly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The first trimester is when most miscarriages happen and when there are fewest outward signs of reassurance, so the mind fills the gap with what-ifs. Knowing this is a normal response, not a premonition, can itself take some of the edge off. Importantly, everyday worry does not cause miscarriage.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'A few things tend to help: focusing on what you can do (attending scans, taking your supplements, resting) rather than what you cannot control, limiting anxious late-night searching, and letting each reassuring milestone, like a heartbeat on a scan, count. Talking to your partner, a trusted person, or your doctor about the fear often lightens it. If the worry becomes constant, stops you sleeping or eating, or tips into panic or persistent low mood, that is worth sharing with your doctor.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Speak to your doctor if anxiety becomes overwhelming or persistent; support and, if needed, further help are available.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Anxiety in early pregnancy is often love arriving before certainty does; being kind to yourself through these weeks matters as much as any checklist.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'First-trimester miscarriage worry is very common and understandable.',
        'Worry itself does not cause miscarriage.',
        'Focus on what you can control; limit anxious searching.',
        'Let reassuring milestones, like a heartbeat, count.',
        'Tell your doctor if anxiety becomes overwhelming.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-011',
      'PREG-EMOTION-001',
      'PREG-BABY-010',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-DRINK-005',
    number: 109,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Are herbal teas and kadha safe in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Nutrition (Can I > Drink)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Drink',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Some herbal teas and a mild kadha are fine in moderation in pregnancy, but "herbal" and "natural" do not automatically mean safe. A few herbs are best avoided or limited, so it is worth checking with your doctor before making any herbal drink a daily habit.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Herbs are active substances, and in strong or medicinal amounts some can affect the pregnancy, while many simply have not been well studied in pregnant women. Gentle options like ginger, or mild tulsi, lemon or fennel-cumin-coriander water, are generally considered fine in small amounts and can even ease nausea or digestion. Ones to be cautious with include licorice (mulethi), aloe vera taken internally, and strong or concentrated infusions; ashwagandha is traditionally used in Ayurveda but some guidance advises caution, which is exactly why a quick word with your doctor helps. A home kadha can be much stronger than a cup of tea, so amount matters.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Keep herbal teas mild and occasional (a cup or two), favour familiar culinary herbs in normal amounts, and avoid strong medicinal brews unless your doctor or a qualified practitioner approves. When buying, choose trusted brands, and treat any herb used to "treat" a symptom as something to clear with your doctor first.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Check with your doctor before using any medicinal herb, strong kadha, or herbal tea regularly.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Traditional remedies carry real wisdom, and also real potency; using them gently and with guidance honours both their value and your baby\'s safety.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Some herbal teas and mild kadha are fine in moderation.',
        '"Natural" does not automatically mean safe in pregnancy.',
        'Gentle: ginger, mild tulsi, lemon, fennel-cumin-coriander water.',
        'Be cautious with licorice (mulethi), internal aloe, strong infusions; check on ashwagandha.',
        'Ask your doctor before any medicinal herb or daily herbal habit.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-CANI-EAT-012',
    number: 110,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Are dry fruits and nuts good in pregnancy?',
    category: 'Food (Can I > Eat)',
    applicability: 'All trimesters',
    canI: 'Eat',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Dry fruits and nuts are a genuinely good addition to a pregnancy diet, eaten in sensible amounts. Almonds, walnuts, dates, figs, raisins and pistachios offer protein, fibre, iron and healthy fats.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'These little foods are nutrient-dense: nuts add protein and healthy fats (walnuts bring omega-3, useful for your baby\'s brain development), while dates, figs and raisins provide iron and fibre that help with the anaemia and constipation common in Indian pregnancies. Because they are also calorie-dense, a handful a day is plenty rather than a bowlful.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Keep a small daily mix as a snack; many women soak almonds or raisins overnight, which is fine and can be gentler on digestion. If you have gestational diabetes, are managing weight, or have a nut allergy, check portions or suitability with your doctor. There is no need for expensive "special" mixes; everyday dry fruits do the job.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Check with your doctor if you have a nut allergy, gestational diabetes, or concerns about weight gain.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A modest handful of dry fruits is one of the simplest, most traditional ways to add quiet nutrition to your day.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Dry fruits and nuts are nutritious in sensible amounts.',
        'They provide protein, fibre, iron and healthy fats.',
        'Walnuts add omega-3; dates, figs and raisins add iron and fibre.',
        'A handful a day is enough; they are calorie-dense.',
        'Check portions if you have gestational diabetes or a nut allergy.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-EAT-001',
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-003',
      'PREG-HEALTH-006',
    ],
