  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-032',
    number: 111,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Are constipation and gas normal in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester onward',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Constipation and gas are very common in early pregnancy and usually harmless. They happen because pregnancy hormones slow digestion, and iron supplements often add to it. More fibre, fluids and gentle movement usually help; check with your doctor before taking any laxative, and see them if you have severe pain, no relief, or blood.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Rising progesterone relaxes the muscles of the gut, so food moves more slowly, which lets more gas build up and stools become harder. The iron in most Indian prenatal supplements is important for you and your baby but is well known to worsen constipation, and nausea can add to it if you are eating little or swallowing air. A small upside of this slower digestion is that your body has more time to absorb nutrients; it is a normal part of early pregnancy, not a sign anything is wrong.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Build up fibre gradually with whole grains, dals, fruits, vegetables and soaked options like raisins or figs; drink plenty of water through the day; and keep moving with short walks. Smaller, more frequent meals and a daily bowl of curd can help. Do not strain on the toilet, as that can lead to piles. If your iron supplement seems to be the trigger, ask your doctor about adjusting it rather than stopping it yourself, and check with them before using any laxative, stool softener or fibre supplement.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor for severe or ongoing pain, no bowel movement for several days despite these steps, or any blood.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'A sluggish gut is one of early pregnancy\'s least glamorous surprises; small daily habits usually ease it more reliably than any quick fix.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Constipation and gas are common and usually harmless.',
        'Progesterone slows digestion; iron supplements add to it.',
        'Fibre, fluids and gentle movement are the mainstays.',
        'Don\'t strain; ask your doctor before any laxative or before changing iron.',
        'See your doctor for severe pain, no relief, or blood.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-CANI-TAKE-003',
      'PREG-BODY-021',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-033',
    number: 112,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Why do I need to pee so often in early pregnancy?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Needing to pee more often is a normal, early sign of pregnancy, often starting in the first weeks. It is caused by pregnancy changes in your blood flow and kidneys, and by your growing womb. It is only a concern if it comes with burning or pain, which can mean a urine infection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Early on, your blood volume rises and your kidneys work harder, filtering more and making more urine. Pregnancy hormones increase blood flow to the pelvis, and your growing womb sits close to the bladder and presses on it. Interestingly, this pressure often eases for a while towards the end of the first trimester as the womb rises higher, then returns later in pregnancy.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'There is nothing to fix; this is your body doing its job. Do not cut down on water to pee less, since staying hydrated matters; instead, ease off fluids in the hour before bed so your sleep is less broken, and empty your bladder fully each time. What is worth checking is burning, stinging or pain when you pee, cloudy or strong-smelling urine, or a constant urge, which can point to a urine infection.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'See your doctor if peeing comes with burning, pain, cloudy urine or fever, which may be a urinary tract infection (UTI).',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Those extra trips to the bathroom are an early, if inconvenient, sign of just how much your body is already adapting for your baby.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Frequent urination is a normal early sign, often from the first weeks.',
        'Caused by more blood, harder-working kidneys and the growing womb.',
        'It often eases late in the first trimester, then returns later.',
        'Don\'t cut water; just ease off fluids before bed.',
        'Burning or pain when peeing needs checking (possible UTI).',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-HEALTH-016',
      'PREG-BODY-025',
      'PREG-BODY-030',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-BODY-034',
    number: 113,
    month: 2,
    question: 'Are food cravings and aversions normal, and when should I worry?',
    category: 'Body & Symptoms',
    applicability: 'First to second trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'Food cravings and sudden dislikes (aversions) are a normal, very common part of early pregnancy, driven mostly by hormones affecting your taste and smell. They are harmless in themselves. The one thing to flag to your doctor is a craving for non-food things like mud, chalk, raw rice or ice.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'Rising pregnancy hormones, including hCG (human chorionic gonadotropin), heighten your sense of smell and taste, which can make you long for certain foods and be put off by others, often the same ones you used to enjoy. Aversions frequently travel with nausea and may be the body\'s way of steering you away from strong or risky smells. None of this means anything is wrong, and having no cravings at all is just as normal.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Enjoy reasonable cravings in moderation, and work around aversions by eating whatever nourishing foods you can tolerate, bland, cold or simple is fine when smells are hard. The important exception is pica, a craving to eat non-food substances such as soil, clay, chalk, ash or large amounts of ice; this can be linked to low iron and can be harmful, so tell your doctor, who may check your iron levels.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'Tell your doctor if you crave or find yourself eating non-food items, or if aversions leave you unable to eat much at all.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Cravings and aversions can feel baffling, but they are simply your heightened senses reacting to a body in the middle of remarkable change.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'Cravings and aversions are common and usually harmless.',
        'They come from hormones heightening taste and smell.',
        'Aversions often go with nausea; eat what you can tolerate.',
        'No cravings at all is also normal.',
        'Craving non-food items (pica) needs a doctor\'s check, often for iron.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-BODY-015',
      'PREG-HEALTH-006',
    ],
  ),
  PregFaq(
    id: 'PREG-EMOTION-008',
    number: 114,
    month: 2,
    question: 'When and how should I tell people I\'m pregnant?',
    category: 'Emotional Wellbeing / Partner & Family',
    applicability: 'First trimester',
    canI: 'Not applicable',
    sensitivity: 'No',
    parts: [
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.direct, '', [
        'There is no single right time to share your pregnancy news; it is entirely your choice. Many people wait until after the 12-week scan, when the risk of miscarriage has dropped, while others tell close family or their employer earlier. Do what feels right and supportive for you.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.why, '', [
        'The common "wait till 12 weeks" custom exists because most miscarriages happen in the first trimester, and some prefer to have that reassurance before sharing widely. But there is no rule. Telling a few trusted people early can mean support through nausea, tiredness and any worries, and telling your employer may matter if your work involves long hours, travel or anything that affects your comfort or safety.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.forYou, '', [
        'Think about who you would want beside you if the early weeks turned difficult, and who you can tell for practical support now, versus the wider circle you might tell after a reassuring scan. In many Indian families, close elders are told early; you can still set your own pace with the extended family and workplace. There is no need to announce anything before you are ready.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.doctor, '', [
        'There is nothing medical to decide here; your doctor can advise on informing your employer if your work carries specific risks.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.perspective, '', [
        'Sharing your news is a personal milestone, not a deadline; the right time is simply whenever it brings you more support and less pressure.',
      ], []),
      PregFaqPart(PregFaqSection.takeaways, '', [], [
        'There is no single right time; it is your choice.',
        'Many wait until after the 12-week scan; others tell close people earlier.',
        'Early support through nausea and worry can be a good reason to tell a few.',
        'Consider telling your employer if work affects your comfort or safety.',
        'Set your own pace with wider family and workplace.',
      ]),
    ],
    related: [
      'PREG-EMOTION-006',
      'PREG-BODY-031',
      'PREG-EMOTION-007',
